{"name":"Spreadsheet Green","hex":"#007711","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spreadsheet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A computer application for organization, analysis, and storage of data in tabular form.","A document created with such an application."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To model or compute by means of a spreadsheet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spreadsheet"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spreadsheet","blocks":[{"text":"A spreadsheet is a computer application for computation, organization, analysis and storage of data in tabular form. Spreadsheets were developed as computerized analogs of paper accounting worksheets. The program operates on data entered in cells of a table. Each cell may contain either numeric or text data, or the results of formulas that automatically calculate and display a value based on the contents of other cells. The term spreadsheet may also refer to one such electronic document."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/LibreOffice_Calc_6.4.png/330px-LibreOffice_Calc_6.4.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spreadsheet"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Python_demo_-_spreadsheet.png/330px-Python_demo_-_spreadsheet.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Python_demo_-_spreadsheet.png/330px-Python_demo_-_spreadsheet.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APython_demo_-_spreadsheet.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Visicalc.png/500px-Visicalc.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Visicalc.png/500px-Visicalc.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"VisiCalc running on an Apple II","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVisicalc.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Named_Variables_in_Excel.PNG/500px-Named_Variables_in_Excel.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Named_Variables_in_Excel.PNG/500px-Named_Variables_in_Excel.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Use of named column variables x & y in Microsoft Excel. Formula for y=x2 resembles Fortran, and Name Manager shows the definitions of x & y.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANamed_Variables_in_Excel.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Spreadsheet_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Spreadsheet_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Animation of a simple spreadsheet that multiplies values in the left column by 2, then sums the calculated values from the right column to the bottom-most cell. In this example, only the values in the A column are entered (10, 20, 30), and the remainder of cells are formulas. Formulas in the B column multiply values from the A column using relative references, and the formula in B4 uses the SUM() function to find the sum of values in the B1:B3 range.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpreadsheet_animation.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Functions_in_Excel.PNG/500px-Functions_in_Excel.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Functions_in_Excel.PNG/500px-Functions_in_Excel.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Use of user-defined function sq(x) in Microsoft Excel ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFunctions_in_Excel.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Subroutine_in_Excel.PNG/500px-Subroutine_in_Excel.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"http